 17592186045417] [17592186143304 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7740ee8-6b81-5e7a-9844-9d92f4f6b3e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143304 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{2}{9}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{2}{9}=\\frac{(1\\times 9)+2}{9}=\\frac{11}{9}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{9}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($3$) and the second fraction's denominator ($9$) have a common \nfactor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{9} & =\\frac{3}{9}\\times \\frac{11}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times 11 \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $11.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143670 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186143671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143670 #edb/ref 1061 135662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143670 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ff49a4a6-b4f3-58d2-abaf-f361c173cdda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143670 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186144068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143671 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186143672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143671 #edb/ref 1061 135663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143671 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2edcdc31-c974-59ed-842e-0a95124a2b70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143671 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186142798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143672 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186143673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143672 #edb/ref 1061 135667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143672 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4da2ef37-0079-538c-a9e6-9975c849c5d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143672 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186142799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143673 #edb/ref 1061 135671 #edb/ref 1759218