tion problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{19}{8}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($4$) and the second fraction's denominator ($8$) have a common \nfactor of $4.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{19}{8} & =\\frac{4}{8}\\times \\frac{19}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times 19 \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 19}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $19.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433041 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433041 #edb/ref 1069 "q-211240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433041 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433041 #edb/ref 1071 "$4\\times 2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433041 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d97e614-0772-528b-a353-38b67fdb040e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433041 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{4}=\\frac{4\\times 11}{1\\times 4}=\\frac{44}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{44}{4}=\\frac{44÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{11}{1}=11\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-211247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433042 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as a whole number, $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 4=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0e3e224